On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:59:36 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:

> +    jj --quiet commit -m "${prefix}${subject:-(no subject)}" "root-file:\"${f}\""
>
> Why is root-file used here? I've never seen that before.

jj reads each path given to commit as a fileset expression, not as a
literal path, and maildir filenames break that parse. The ":2," flag
suffix uses both ":", which introduces a pattern kind, and ",", which
is not a legal bare character. A bare filename fails:

    $ jj diff --summary -r @- maildir/cur/1791623264.P20539ffb6b1.beastie:2,
    Error: Failed to parse fileset: Syntax error
      |
    1 | maildir/cur/1791623264.P20539ffb6b1.beastie:2,
      |                                            ^---

So the path has to be a quoted string, and a quoted string needs a
pattern kind in front of it. root-file:"path" matches that one exact
file, relative to the workspace root (see "jj help -k filesets").

I picked root-file over plain file: because file: is relative to the
current directory, and `jj st` prints paths relative to the current
directory too. The two only agree because just runs the recipe from
the Justfile's directory, the repo root. root-file states that the
paths are root-relative instead of relying on where just puts us.
That said, given just's working directory, file:"${f}" would behave
the same. If you prefer it as the more familiar spelling, I can send
a follow-up patch.

Thanks for applying it.
